<div class="comp204-page">
  <h2>COMP204 — Distributed Systems</h2>
  <p class="comp204-subtitle">Falmouth University · AJ313798</p>

  <p class="comp204-intro">
    Bloc started as a solo web project. For COMP204 it was extended into a distributed system:
    a shared backend serving two independent clients — a server-rendered website and a React Native
    mobile app — over the same REST API. This page documents what existed before the module and
    what was built for it.
  </p>

  <div class="comp204-grid">

    <div class="comp204-col original">
      <h3>Original project</h3>
      <p class="comp204-col-desc">Web-only bouldering session tracker</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Express + Handlebars server-rendered site</li>
        <li>Cookie / express-session authentication</li>
        <li>Start, view, and end bouldering sessions</li>
        <li>Log individual climbs with grade, attempts, zones, topped flag, notes, and photo</li>
        <li>Public feed of completed sessions</li>
        <li>Multer file upload to <code>/public/uploads/</code></li>
        <li>SQLite database with <code>users</code>, <code>sessions</code>, <code>climbs</code> tables</li>
        <li>All logic in a single <code>server.js</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="comp204-col added">
      <h3>Added for COMP204</h3>
      <p class="comp204-col-desc">Distributed system extensions</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>REST API</strong> (<code>/api/*</code>) — a separate JSON API consumed by the
          mobile client, mounted alongside the existing web routes
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>JWT authentication</strong> — <code>requireApiAuth</code> middleware validates
          Bearer tokens for every API route; the web app continues to use cookies so both auth
          mechanisms coexist on the same server
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CORS</strong> — enables the mobile app to call the API from a different origin
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Modular structure</strong> — routes and middleware refactored from a single file
          into <code>routes/api.js</code>, <code>routes/web.js</code>, and <code>middleware/</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>React Native mobile app</strong> — Feed, My Sessions, and Stats tabs; login /
          register; session detail with add-climb form, grade picker, and photo upload; JWT stored
          in AuthContext
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stats</strong> — <code>GET /api/stats</code> aggregates total sessions, climbs,
          topped percentage, average attempts, grade distribution, and favourite gym; mirrored as
          a <a href="/profile">Profile</a> page on the web
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Delete climb</strong> — <code>DELETE /api/sessions/:id/climbs/:climbId</code>
          (API) and a POST equivalent for HTML forms on the web
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grade picker</strong> — mobile add-climb form replaced free-text input with a
          modal selector covering V-scale (VB–V17) and Fontainebleau (4–8C+)
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div>

  <div class="comp204-arch">
    <h3>Architecture</h3>
    <pre class="arch-diagram">
 React Native app  ──┐
                     ├──▶  Express server (Node.js)  ──▶  SQLite DB
 Web browser       ──┘          │
                                └──▶  /public/uploads/  (images)

 Mobile auth:  JWT Bearer token   (stateless, stored in AuthContext)
 Web auth:     express-session cookie  (server-side session store)
    </pre>
    <p>
      Both clients read and write the same database in real time. A climb logged on the mobile app
      appears immediately on the web feed, and vice versa — demonstrating the core distributed
      systems requirement of shared state across independent clients.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>
